2:id: cor-metrizability-and-first-countability-are-hereditary
4:title: "Every subspace of a metrizable space is metrizable and every subspace of a first countable space is first countable, the metric case being the subspace metric already identified with the subspace topology"
38:## Statement
39:
40:Both of the following properties of topological spaces are **hereditary**
41:([[def-hereditary-property]]).
42:
43:1. **Metrizability** ([[def-metrizable-space]]). If $\mathcal{T}$ is induced by a
44:   metric $d$ on $X$ and $S \subseteq X$, then the subspace topology
45:   $\mathcal{T}_S$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) is induced by the subspace
46:   metric $d_S = d \restriction (S \times S)$
47:   ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). So a subspace of a metrizable space
48:   is metrizable, and a metric inducing its topology is available explicitly and
49:   not merely asserted to exist.
50:2. **First countability** ([[def-first-countable-top]]). If every point of $X$
51:   has an at most countable neighbourhood base and $S \subseteq X$, then every
52:   point of $S$ has an at most countable neighbourhood base in
53:   $(S, \mathcal{T}_S)$, namely the family of traces on $S$ of the members of a
54:   base at that point in $X$.
55:
56:Claim 1 is a corollary in the strict sense: the identification of the subspace
57:topology with the metric topology of the subspace metric is discharged inside
58:[[def-metrizable-space]], and nothing is reproved here.
