2:id: def-filter
4:title: "Filter on a set"
41:## Definition
42:
43:Let $X$ be a set. A family $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ of subsets of
44:$X$ is a **filter on $X$** when it satisfies:
45:
46:- **(F1)** *nontriviality*: $X \in \mathcal{F}$;
47:- **(F2)** *properness*: $\emptyset \notin \mathcal{F}$;
48:- **(F3)** *closure under pairwise intersection*: if $A, B \in \mathcal{F}$ then
49:  $A \cap B \in \mathcal{F}$;
50:- **(F4)** *upward closure in $X$*: if $A \in \mathcal{F}$ and
51:  $A \subseteq B \subseteq X$ then $B \in \mathcal{F}$.
52:
53:The set of all filters on $X$ is written $\mathrm{Filt}(X)$. It is a subset of
54:$\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(X))$, hence a set, and it is ordered by inclusion:
55:$\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{G}$ is read "$\mathcal{G}$ is **finer** than
56:$\mathcal{F}$", and $\mathcal{F}$ is **coarser** than $\mathcal{G}$.
57:
58:**Convention: filters are proper.** Condition (F2) is part of the definition
59:throughout this library, so "filter" always means "proper filter". The competing
60:convention drops (F2), calls the resulting objects filters, and says **proper
61:filter** for one that omits $\emptyset$. The two conventions differ by exactly one
62:object, since (F4) forces any family satisfying (F1), (F3) and (F4) that contains
63:$\emptyset$ to be all of $\mathcal{P}(X)$: if $\emptyset \in \mathcal{F}$ then
64:$\emptyset \subseteq B \subseteq X$ gives $B \in \mathcal{F}$ for every
65:$B \subseteq X$. That single extra object is the **improper filter**
66:$\mathcal{P}(X)$. This library follows the more widely adopted convention, in
67:which the improper filter is not a filter; a reader arriving from the other
68:convention should read every unqualified "filter" below as "proper filter".
69:

