2:id: def-finite-intersection-property
4:title: "Finite intersection property"
39:## Definition
40:
41:Let $X$ be a set and $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ a family of subsets of
42:$X$. A **finite list** in $\mathcal{A}$ is a function $s : n \to \mathcal{A}$ for
43:some $n \in \mathbb{N}$, where $n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is its own set of
44:predecessors in the von Neumann encoding ([[def-natural-numbers]]). Its
45:**intersection** is the subset of $X$
46:
47:$$\textstyle\bigcap_{i \in n} s(i) := \{\, x \in X \;:\; x \in s(i) \text{ for every } i \in n \,\},$$
48:
49:a definition by Separation alone, with no recursion involved. For $n = 0$, that is
50:$n = \emptyset$, the condition is vacuous and the **empty intersection** is $X$.
51:
52:The family $\mathcal{A}$ has the **finite intersection property**, abbreviated
53:**FIP**, when
54:
55:$$\textstyle\bigcap_{i \in n} s(i) \neq \emptyset \quad \text{for every } n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and every finite list } s : n \to \mathcal{A}.$$
56:
57:Equivalently: no finitely many members of $\mathcal{A}$ have empty intersection.
