2:id: def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps
4:title: "Homeomorphism, open map, closed map, embedding, and what it means for a property to be topological"
36:## Definition
37:
38:Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces and let
39:$f : X \to Y$ be a function. Continuity is as in [[def-continuous-map-top]],
40:injections, surjections and bijections as in
41:[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]].
42:
43:- $f$ is an **open map** if $f[U]$ is open in $Y$ for every open $U \subseteq X$.
44:- $f$ is a **closed map** if $f[F]$ is closed in $Y$ for every closed
45:  $F \subseteq X$.
46:- $f$ is a **homeomorphism** if $f$ is a continuous bijection whose inverse
47:  $f^{-1} : Y \to X$ is also continuous. The spaces are **homeomorphic**, written
48:  $X \cong Y$, when a homeomorphism $X \to Y$ exists.
49:- $f$ is an **embedding** if $f$ is injective and the corestriction
50:  $f_0 : X \to f[X]$, $f_0(x) = f(x)$, is a homeomorphism onto $f[X]$ carrying
51:  the subspace topology inherited from $Y$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
52:
53:**The inverse in the third clause exists because $f$ is a bijection**, and it is
54:the unique two-sided inverse ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]); no choice
55:principle is involved. Continuity of $f^{-1}$ is a genuine additional demand: a
56:continuous bijection need not be a homeomorphism, and this page records that
57:failure as a false statement with a two-point witness.
58:
59:**Open, closed and homeomorphism are three different conditions.** A
60:homeomorphism is continuous by definition, but an open map need not be continuous
61:and a closed map need not be continuous; and continuity implies neither openness
62:nor closedness. An open map need
63:not be closed and a closed map need not be open, and Sierpinski space
64:$S = \{a,b\}$ with open point $b$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]) witnesses both
65:failures at once: the constant map $S \to S$ with value $b$ is open, since the
66:image of every nonempty set is the open set $\{b\}$, and is not closed, since the
67:image of the closed set $\{a\}$ is $\{b\}$, whose complement $\{a\}$ is not open;
68:the constant map with value $a$ is closed and not open by the same computation
69:read the other way. What is true
70:is that for a **continuous bijection** the three notions collapse: it is a
71:homeomorphism exactly when it is open, exactly when it is closed. That is proved
72:in the next item and is not assumed here.
73:
74:**Topological properties.** A **property of topological spaces** is a condition
75:$P$ that is either true or false of each space. $P$ is a **topological property**
76:(one is also said to be *preserved by homeomorphism*, or *invariant*) when
77:$X \cong Y$ implies that $P(X)$ and $P(Y)$ have the same truth value. Since
78:$\cong$ is an equivalence relation on spaces — the identity is a homeomorphism,
79:inverses and composites of homeomorphisms are homeomorphisms, all three verified
80:in the next item — a topological property is exactly one that is constant on each
81:$\cong$-class.
82:
83:**What a homeomorphism transports.** If $h : X \to Y$ is a homeomorphism then
84:$U \mapsto h[U]$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{T}_X$ onto $\mathcal{T}_Y$, with
85:inverse $V \mapsto h^{-1}[V]$: both maps are well defined because $h$ and
86:$h^{-1}$ are continuous, and they are mutually inverse because $h$ is a
87:bijection. So a homeomorphism is an isomorphism of the structure "a set together
88:with a distinguished family of subsets", and every notion defined from the open
89:sets alone — closed, closure, interior, boundary, dense, convergence of
90:sequences, continuity of maps into and out of the space — is carried across by
91:it. Anything defined from extra data, such as a metric or an order, is not, and
92:that distinction is exactly what the phrase *topological property* is for.
93:

