2:id: def-metric-compactness
4:title: "Open cover, subcover, compact metric space, and compact subset of a metric space"
34:## Definition
35:
36:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with open sets as in
37:[[def-metric-topology]] and balls as in [[def-metric-ball]].
38:
39:- An **open cover** of $(X,d)$ is a family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $X$
40:  with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$, where
41:  $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
42:- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily
43:  $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ that is itself an open cover.
44:- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \emptyset$ or
45:  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
46:  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
47:  harmless.
48:- $(X,d)$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite subcover: for
49:  every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \emptyset$ and the empty subfamily
50:  covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
51:  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
52:  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
53:- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the metric
54:  subspace $(A, d_A)$ is a compact metric space, $d_A$ being the restriction of
55:  $d$ to $A \times A$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]).
56:
57:**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
58:The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A,d_A)$ and its own open sets, not
59:about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do agree,
60:but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
61:[[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]], and no item of this library may use the
62:ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading as the
63:definition is what makes "compact" a property of the metric space $(A,d_A)$
64:alone, so that a set compact in one ambient space is compact in every other one
65:containing it isometrically.
66:
67:**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
68:it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. The
69:one-point space is compact too, and so is every space listed as
70:$\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$: given a cover, each $x_i$ lies in some member, and
71:finitely many members chosen in this way already cover.
72:
73:**The finiteness convention, and how it is used both ways.** "Finite" above is
74:the listing form, matching the finite lists of
75:[[def-finite-intersection-property]]. It agrees with the definition of
76:finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number ([[def-countable]]), and
77:both directions of the agreement are available and are used below:
78:
79:- A nonempty finite set $F$ in the sense of [[def-countable]] satisfies
80:  $F \approx m$ for some $m \ge 1$, and a bijection $m \to F$ is exactly a
81:  listing $F = \{a_0, \dots, a_{m-1}\}$.
82:- Conversely a set listed as $A = \{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$, that is the image of a
83:  function $a$ with domain $\sigma(n)$, is finite in the sense of
84:  [[def-countable]]: the map sending $x \in A$ to the least $i \le n$ with
85:  $a_i = x$ is an injection of $A$ into $\sigma(n)$, so $A$ is equinumerous with
86:  a subset of $\mathbb{N}$ bounded above, and such a subset is finite
87:  ([[lem-subset-of-countable]]).
88:
89:Neither direction uses a choice principle: the second selects nothing, taking a
90:least index instead.
91:

