 succeeded in 0ms:
2:id: def-metric-topology
4:title: "The metric topology: a set is open when every one of its points has a ball around it inside the set; closed means open complement"
37:## Definition
38:
39:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).
40:
41:A subset $U \subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \in U$ there is a
42:real $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset
43:$F \subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \setminus F$ is
44:open.
45:
46:The collection
47:
48:$$\mathcal{T}_d := \{\, U \subseteq X : U \text{ is open in } (X,d) \,\}$$
49:
50:of all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$
51:that is both open and closed is called **clopen**.
52:
53:**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\emptyset$ is open, because the
54:defining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because
55:$B(x,r) \subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and
56:$\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.
57:
58:**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any open set containing $x$. The condition
59:above therefore reads: $U$ is open exactly when every point of $U$ has a ball
60:around it inside $U$, and it is the balls alone that have to be tested.
61:
62:**The metric, not the set, determines $\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same
63:set may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the
64:same one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].
65:

