2:id: lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient
4:title: "A subspace is compact exactly when every family of open subsets of the ambient space covering it, indexed or not, has finitely many members covering it"
31:## Statement
32:
33:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), let
34:$A \subseteq X$ and let $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ be the subspace
35:([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). Then:
36:
37:1. **Compactness read in the ambient space.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$
38:   ([[def-compact-space]]), that is $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact space, if
39:   and only if for every family $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ with
40:   $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
41:   $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$,
42:   or else $A = \varnothing$.
43:2. **The same in indexed form.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$ if and only if
44:   for every set $I$ and every family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ of open subsets of $X$
45:   with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
46:   indices $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with
47:   $A \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, or else $A = \varnothing$.
48:
49:Claim 2 is the form used by almost every later proof on this page, because a
50:cover is usually produced by a rule that attaches an open set to each point or to
51:each index, and a set of open sets forgets that rule. No choice principle is used
52:anywhere below; the one place a selection is made is over a finite index set, and
53:[[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
