2:id: lem-finite-choice
4:title: "Every natural-number-indexed list of nonempty sets has a choice function on its family of values"
34:## Statement
35:
36:Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $F$ be a function with domain $n$ all of whose
37:values are nonempty sets. Then the family of its values,
38:$\mathcal{F} = F[n] = \{\, F(i) : i \in n \,\}$, has a choice function
39:([[def-choice-function]]).
40:
41:This is a theorem of ZF: its proof uses no form of the Axiom of Choice
42:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
43:
44:What is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$. The
45:natural number $n$ serves as the index set in the von Neumann sense,
46:$n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so "$F$ has domain $n$"
47:says precisely that the members of $\mathcal{F}$ are listed as
48:$F(0), \dots, F(n-1)$. The listing need not be injective, and $\mathcal{F}$ is the
49:set of values, so repetitions are harmless and are not counted.
50:
51:The displayed statement and its proof use only a natural-number-indexed
52:function. They do not identify an arbitrary finite family with a particular
53:enumeration.
