2:id: lem-finite-set-has-max
4:title: "Every nonempty finite set of reals has a maximum and a minimum"
38:## Statement
39:
40:For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \dots, a_n \in \mathbb{R}$, the
41:set $\{a_0, a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).
42:
43:What is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.
44:
45:The usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$ has a
46:maximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets
47:of $\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$. That
48:identification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page
49:has no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not
50:merely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two
51:descriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is
52:recorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this
53:lemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses
54:the notation
55:$\max\{a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ and $\min\{a_1, \dots, a_n\}$ for finite sets of
56:**real** numbers from this page onwards.
