2:id: lem-fip-generates-filter
4:title: "A family lies in a filter exactly when it has the finite intersection property"
45:## Statement
46:
47:Let $X$ be a set and $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$. Write
48:
49:$$\mathcal{A}^{\cap} := \{\, \textstyle\bigcap_{i \in n} s(i) \;:\; n \in \mathbb{N},\ s : n \to \mathcal{A} \,\}$$
50:
51:for the family of intersections of finite lists in $\mathcal{A}$
52:([[def-finite-intersection-property]]), which contains $X$ as the empty
53:intersection. Then:
54:
55:1. $\mathcal{A}^{\cap}$ is closed under pairwise intersection and contains
56:   $\mathcal{A} \cup \{X\}$;
57:2. if $\mathcal{A}$ has the finite intersection property, then
58:   $\mathcal{A}^{\cap}$ is a filter base on $X$ ([[def-filter-base]]) and
59:   $\langle \mathcal{A}^{\cap} \rangle$ is the smallest filter on $X$ containing
60:   $\mathcal{A}$ ([[def-filter]], [[lem-filter-base-generates]]);
61:3. conversely, if $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ for some filter $\mathcal{F}$
62:   on $X$, then $\mathcal{A}$ has the finite intersection property.
63:
64:Consequently a family of subsets of $X$ is contained in some filter on $X$ if and
65:only if it has the finite intersection property.
