    23	pipeline_run: null
    24	---
    25	
    26	## Definition
    27	
    28	A real $x$ is **positive** when it has a representative $(a_n)$ together
    29	with a rational $\delta > 0$ and an index $N$ such that $a_n > \delta$
    30	for all $n \ge N$. Define
    31	
    32	$$x \le y \iff x = y \;\text{ or }\; y - x \text{ is positive},$$
    33	
    34	and $|x| = x$ if $x \ge 0$, $|x| = -x$ otherwise.
    35	
    36	## Remarks
    37	
    38	- Independence of the representative, trichotomy, and compatibility with the
    39	  field operations: [[thm-reals-ordered-field]].
    40	- The triangle inequality for this $|\cdot|$ holds because the proof of
    41	  [[lem-rat-triangle]] uses only the axioms of a totally ordered field.
     1	---
     2	id: def-complete-ordered-field
     3	kind: definition
     4	title: "Complete ordered field (least-upper-bound property)"
     5	status: published
     6	origin: session
     7	provenance:
     8	  statement: literature-derived
     9	  proof: not-applicable
    10	deps: [def-ordered-field]
    11	aliases: [def-dedekind-complete-field]
    12	landmark: true
    13	short: "complete ordered field"
    14	verification:
    15	  precheck: n/a
    16	  audited: 2026-07-25
    17	sources:
    18	  scraped: []
    19	  references:
    20	    - title: "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Ch. 1"
    21	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principles_of_Mathematical_Analysis"
    22	    - title: "M. Spivak, Calculus, 4th ed., Ch. 8"
    23	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Spivak"
    24	    - title: "University of Wisconsin Math 521 notes: Real analysis"
    25	      url: "https://people.math.wisc.edu/~jwrobbin/521dir/521.pdf"
    26	pipeline_run: null
    27	---
    28	
    29	## Definition
    30	
    31	Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq F$.
    32	
    33	- $u \in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$; $S$ is
    34	  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.
    35	- $u \in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$
    36	  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.
    37	
    38	$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the
    39	**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty
    40	$S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.
    41	
    42	## Remarks
    43	
    44	- A least upper bound, if it exists, is unique (two least upper bounds are each
    45	  $\le$ the other, so equal by antisymmetry of the order).
    46	- Applying the property to $\{-s : s \in S\}$ yields the dual **greatest lower
    47	  bound** (infimum) property, so the two are equivalent.
    48	- The Dedekind-cut reals have this property by construction
    49	  ([[thm-dedekind-complete]]); the Cauchy-sequence reals acquire it via
    50	  [[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]. This definition is the target for the
    51	  uniqueness theorem [[thm-uniqueness-complete-ordered-field]].

