items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:120:**Claim 2 is the extreme value theorem, and compactness is the whole of it.** No metric, no completeness argument and no sequence appears: the rays $(-\infty, s)$ with $s \in S$ cover a set with no maximum, and a finite subcover of them is impossible because finitely many reals do have a maximum. The metric statement of the same result is [[thm-extreme-value-metric]], proved there for a compact metric space; by [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] it is the present claim applied to a metric topology.
