items/fs-local-compactness-is-hereditary.md:61:[L2] $\mathbb{R}$ is locally compact: for $p \in \mathbb{R}$ the set $[p-1, p+1]$ is closed and bounded, hence a compact subset of the metric space $\mathbb{R}$ ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]], claim 3) and so a compact subset of the topological space $\mathbb{R}$ ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]], claim 2); and it contains the open $(p-1, p+1) \ni p$, so it is a neighbourhood of $p$ ([[def-neighbourhood-top]], [[def-locally-compact-space]]).
