    24	---
    25	Compactness in metric spaces turns arbitrary open covers into finite data and
    26	supports the finite-intersection method. The page relates compactness to
    27	countable, sequential and limit-point compactness, completeness and total
    28	boundedness. It records the proof cost where it occurs: several implications are
    29	choice-free, constructing an indefinitely separated sequence uses dependent
    30	choice, and fixing finite nets at every scale uses countable choice. Compactness
    31	of a subset is treated intrinsically, so ambient and subspace cover arguments
    32	agree.
    33	
    34	The development proves Heine--Borel in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and then applies
    35	compactness to continuous images, extreme values, Lebesgue numbers, uniform
    36	continuity and continuous inverses. It also obtains a countable dense subset
    37	under countable choice. Explicit counterexamples separate boundedness, total
    38	boundedness and compactness outside Euclidean space, while the choice ledger
    39	states which principles the displayed proofs use without asserting independence
    40	or necessity.

