2:id: thm-compact-iff-finite-intersection-property
4:title: "A metric space is compact if and only if every family of closed subsets with the finite intersection property has nonempty intersection"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with closed sets as in
38:[[def-metric-topology]] and the finite intersection property as in
39:[[def-finite-intersection-property]]. For a family $\mathcal{A}$ of subsets of
40:$X$ write
41:
42:$$\bigcap \mathcal{A} \;:=\; \{\, x \in X : x \in F \text{ for every } F \in \mathcal{A} \,\},$$
43:
44:so that $\bigcap \emptyset = X$, matching the convention for the empty finite
45:intersection in [[def-finite-intersection-property]].
46:
47:Then $(X,d)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]) **if and only if** every
48:family $\mathcal{A}$ of closed subsets of $X$ with the finite intersection
49:property satisfies $\bigcap \mathcal{A} \ne \emptyset$.
50:
51:No choice principle is used in either direction: complementation is a canonical
52:bijection, so no member of a family ever has to be selected.
