41:## Definition
42:
43:Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field
44:([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) constructed in this
45:library ([[def-real-numbers]]) and carrying its order ([[def-real-order]]).
46:
47:Let $X$ be a set. A **metric** on $X$ is a function
48:$d : X \times X \to \mathbb{R}$ such that for all $x, y, z \in X$:
49:
50:- **(M1) Separation.** $d(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $x = y$.
51:- **(M2) Symmetry.** $d(x,y) = d(y,x)$.
52:- **(M3) Triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \le d(x,y) + d(y,z)$.
53:
54:A **metric space** is a pair $(X,d)$ consisting of a set $X$ and a metric $d$ on
55:it. The elements of $X$ are its **points** and $d(x,y)$ is the **distance** from
56:$x$ to $y$. When only one metric is in play we write $X$ for $(X,d)$; when
57:several are, the metric is always named.
58:
59:**The values of a metric are real numbers.** The codomain is $\mathbb{R}$, so
60:$d(x,y)$ is an honest element of the complete ordered field and every
61:inequality above is an inequality there. No infinite value is permitted;
62:[[rem-metric-axiom-conventions]] records why extended metrics are not treated in
63:this library.
64:
65:**Nonnegativity is deliberately absent from the axiom list.** Many texts add a
66:fourth axiom $d(x,y) \ge 0$. It is redundant: (M1), (M2) and (M3) already force
67:it, as [[lem-metric-nonnegativity]] proves. Nothing below assumes it before that
68:lemma is available.
69:
70:**Pseudometric.** A **pseudometric** on $X$ is a function
71:$p : X \times X \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying (M2), (M3) and the weakening
72:
73:- **(M1') Reflexivity.** $p(x,x) = 0$ for every $x \in X$
74:
75:of (M1). A pseudometric may therefore assign distance $0$ to two distinct
76:points. Every metric is a pseudometric, and a pseudometric is a metric exactly
77:when $p(x,y) = 0$ forces $x = y$.
78:
79:**Ultrametric.** An **ultrametric** on $X$ is a metric $d$ that in addition
80:satisfies
81:
82:- **(M3') Strong triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \le \max\{d(x,y), d(y,z)\}$
83:
84:for all $x, y, z \in X$, where the maximum is that of a two-element subset of
85:$\mathbb{R}$, which exists and is one of the two elements
86:([[def-max-min]], [[lem-finite-set-has-max]]). An **ultrametric space** is a pair
87:$(X,d)$ with $d$ an ultrametric.
